have you ever walked out of a building  on a sunny day and had an uncontrollable  urge to sneeze  [Music]  if you answered yes like me you likely  suffer from a strange genetic condition  that has remained unexplained by medical  science for millennia  and if you answered no you probably have  no idea what i'm talking about  sun sneezing as it's more commonly known  is a medical condition called the photic  sneeze reflex another name that some  have given is hold on before we get into  all that this is dr louis patacek a  neurologist and human geneticist at the  university of california san francisco  okay go ahead another name that some  have given is autosomal dominant  compelling  heliophthalmic outburst or achoo  syndrome but psr is really the  appropriate name that we should all use  photic sneeze reflexes a tendency to  sneeze when suddenly exposed to bright  light  and as strange as that may sound it's  more common than you might think i  estimate that about one in ten or ten  percent of all people have this  condition  so a lot of us are affected but how and  why it happens is not fully understood  despite the fact that humans have been  pondering it for thousands of years  in 350 bc aristotle posed the question  why does the heat of the sun provoke  sneezing  aristotle was barking up the wrong tree  though  this doesn't make sense because  heat in and of itself doesn't cause  sneezing some 2000 years later the  philosopher sir francis bacon also tried  to figure it out his theory was that  light from the sun caused your eyes to  water which then seep into your nose  triggering a sneeze  it's an interesting thought but it  doesn't make a lot of sense psr occurs  almost instantaneously and it takes  longer for the tear ducts to react  furthermore tears  don't cause sneezing  there's no evidence for that so modern  medicine has come up with its own  theories and it has to do with the  wiring in your head sneezes are  triggered typically by irritating things  in our nose and these are detected by a  nerve the trigeminal nerve and right  next door to the trigeminal nerve is the  optic nerve you may have heard of this  one the optic nerve is stimulated by  bright light and sends electric signals  to the brain some of that signal can be  picked up by the trigeminal nerve and  misinterpreted by the brain as an  irritation in the nose which triggers  you guessed it a sneeze well that's the  theory anyway photic sneeze reflex is  not a disease consequently the medical  community hasn't paid a whole lot of  attention to it therefore we know very  little about what causes it but dr  pitacic believes that understanding psr  could potentially shed some light on  more serious conditions  epileptic seizures can be caused by  strobing lights migraine headache is  precipitated by light if we can  understand what the mechanism is that  leads to psr this may give insights into  other conditions which actually are  medically important and represent  disorders so there you have it this 2000  year old medical mystery may be worth  solving after all
 

